    "docs/operations.md": {
      "type": "file",
      "sha256": "d6ebf2c0a442a64343ef2d28aed586c4e50f7b647fc422eb27f08d23236aec7c",
      "executable": false,
      "encoding": "utf8",
      "content": "# Harbor operations\n\nContact the platform on-call engineer for incidents. This demonstration service\nhas no authentication and must stay on loopback until a separate security review.\n\n## Startup\n\nFrom the repository root, start Harbor on its default port with:\n\n```sh\npython3 service/server.py\n```\n\nThe process listens only on `127.0.0.1:8765` and runs in the foreground. Set an\nalternate port for a local instance with `PORT=18765 python3 service/server.py`;\n`PORT` must be an integer accepted by the host. A non-integer value terminates\nstartup with a Python `ValueError`, while an unavailable address or port produces\nan `OSError`. The server does not print a readiness message, so use the health\nprobe below before sending work.\n\n`POST /accept` records one accepted request in process memory and returns HTTP\n204. For example:\n\n```sh\ncurl --fail --request POST http://127.0.0.1:8765/accept\n```\n\n## Health\n\nProbe the running process from the same host:\n\n```sh\ncurl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8765/health\n```\n\nA healthy new process returns HTTP 200 with `Content-Type: application/json` and\na response such as:\n\n```json\n{\"status\": \"ok\", \"accepted\": 0}\n```\n\nThe `accepted` number increases after successful `POST /accept` requests. A\nconnection refusal or a nonzero `curl --fail` exit means the probe did not\nestablish health and should be escalated to the platform on-call engineer.\nUnknown paths return HTTP 404. This endpoint checks only the local Python process;\nit does not certify deployment, external reachability, or a downstream system.\n\n## Recovery\n\nStop the foreground process with `Ctrl-C`, correct the reported startup failure\nif any, and restart it with the Startup command. Then wait for `/health` to return\nHTTP 200 before resuming requests.\n\nThe accepted-request counter exists only in process memory. Every restart resets\nit to zero, so a restart loses the previous count. Harbor has no persistent data\nstore, backup, replay, or automated recovery mechanism. Recovery of a lost count\nis unsupported; contact the platform on-call engineer rather than claiming or\nattempting data restoration.\n"
    },
    "docs/operating-status.json": {
